#
# How it plays, in this order (each line is also a limit on what the run proves):
# 1. THE MARKER. The game's own (the map screen's _guide_spot). The script walks to it with real steps (state.step) over
#    tiles the game says are open (a breadth-first search with the game's own tests: ledges, Surf, spin tiles, currents,
#    warp pads), through trainers (their battle) and boulders (Strength's puzzle), and walks into / onto it.
#    A marker on a way out it can't walk straight to: through the gatehouse or cave in between (still "marker").
# 2. THE JOURNAL'S WORDS, when the marker is missing or did nothing new: the place the mission names (its target, the
#    town it must reach, its Gym's town). The script walks toward it over the maps' own ways out, trying each passage of a
#    cave once and backing out of dead ends ("words"). Written down as a Journal gap.
# 3. WANDERING there: what the Journal's words name first (the Gym, the Poke Mart), then people, doors, stairs, nearest
#    first ("wander"). Vermilion Gym's trash cans are searched one by one ("puzzle").
# 4. STUCK: nothing left, or 36 things tried with nothing in the story moving. Written down with a screenshot, then the
#    smallest SKIP: put the player at the mission's place; then beside its own step; as a last resort its own flag /
#    badge / item set by hand (the whole mission skipped). No story flag is ever set before the stuck point is recorded.
# - Battles are counted as won through the game's own end of battle (player_won + exit_battle, as kanto_maps_body does):
#   badges, HMs, flags and rewards are handed out by game code. Gym minigames, slide puzzles and quiz puzzles are counted
#   as solved the same way (their own "done" call). Wild Pokemon are taken off the map: nothing is caught.
# - Shops, the PC, the Day Care, the Game Corner's machines and tutors' lists are not used (a list: BACK).
# - A yes / no question is answered yes. A hover menu: the gold option, else each in turn.
# - If the game never comes back to the player (9000 frames) that is a HANG: recorded, then the screen is closed by the
#   script so the run can go on. One known cause, game.transitioning left True by a League battle, is cleared from then on.
